WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.510
Three pods of a payment service share one Redis server.

2
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:11.844
In its memory, Redis keeps a counter: card seven has 41 authorizations today.

3
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:15.743
The obvious design gives every connection its own thread.

4
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:19.978
Now pods A and B both add one to the counter.

5
00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:23.087
Both threads read 41, at the same moment.

6
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.599
Both add one and write back 42.

7
00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:29.009
Two adds, but the counter only went up by one.

8
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:33.571
To stop that, every key needs a lock, and every command pays for it.

9
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.715
Redis does something simpler.

10
00:00:35.715 --> 00:00:37.696
One thread runs every command.

11
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.216
The thread asks the kernel which connections have a command waiting.

12
00:00:42.216 --> 00:00:44.116
Then it takes them one at a time.

13
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:48.849
It runs A's command to the end: read 41, add one, store 42.

14
00:00:48.849 --> 00:00:51.835
Only then does it look at the next one.

15
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.276
B's command runs next.

16
00:00:54.276 --> 00:00:56.531
It reads 42 and stores 43.

17
00:00:56.531 --> 00:00:57.920
Then C reads 43.

18
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.737
No update is lost, and nobody needed a lock.

19
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.682
Commands never overlap, so every command is atomic.

20
00:01:05.682 --> 00:01:09.768
It happens as one step, and no client ever sees it half done.

21
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.481
The same rule has a price.

22
00:01:12.481 --> 00:01:18.347
Pod A now sends a Lua script, a small program that Redis runs as one command.

23
00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:20.632
This one runs for 1.5 seconds.

24
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:22.765
Pod B asks for the counter.

25
00:01:22.765 --> 00:01:27.560
That takes half a microsecond of work, but the only thread is busy.

26
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:28.132
B waits.

27
00:01:28.132 --> 00:01:30.780
So does pod C, whatever key it wants.

28
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.579
When the script ends, B finally gets its answer.

29
00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:36.219
It waited 1.2 seconds.

30
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:39.934
KEYS over a million keys does the same.

31
00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:44.039
Any command whose work grows with the data freezes every client.

32
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.851
One thread, one command at a time, each run to the end.

33
00:01:47.851 --> 00:01:53.261
That makes every command atomic, and it means one slow command makes everyone wait.
